From f25736a03f5b6abbc30eaa9636e4cf3ef21519eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 02:05:03 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Text system: line heights as macOS lays them out, and
 usesFontLeading

GNUstep laid each line out at the font's -defaultLineHeightForFont,
which the cairo backend gives as the font's own line height, ascender,
descender and line gap added unrounded: Arial at 10 points made lines of
11.499 points.  NSFont's -leading was always 0, NSLayoutManager had no
-usesFontLeading, and lineHeightMultiple was ignored.  On macOS 15
(TextKit 1) the same text makes lines of 11.327, or 11.0 when the layout
manager does not use the font leading; measured over Arial, Times New
Roman and Georgia at 9, 10, 12, 14.5 and 20 points, with and without a
paragraph style:

- a line is the ascender and the descender, each rounded to a whole
  point, and its baseline is the rounded ascender below its top
  (Arial 10: 9 + 2, baseline at 9);
- the font's leading (its line gap, 0.327 for Arial 10) goes below that,
  unrounded, when -usesFontLeading is YES, the default;
- lineHeightMultiple scales the rounded height, and the extra goes above
  the baseline (Arial 10 at 1.5: 16.5, baseline at 14.5); minimum and
  maximum line heights then clamp it, as before;
- lineSpacing takes the place of the leading where it is larger, except
  under the last line of the text, which keeps only the leading;
- -defaultLineHeightForFont: is the rounded ascender and descender plus
  the leading rounded, when it is used (Times New Roman 12: 11 + 3 + 1),
  and the extra line fragment after a final newline is that high;
- string drawing (-sizeWithAttributes:, -boundingRectWithSize:options:,
  -drawInRect:...) leaves the leading out unless the options include
  NSStringDrawingUsesFontLeading.

Make the typesetter do the same, give NSFont a -leading (the backend's
line height less the ascender and descender, so 0 where the backend has
no line gap), add -usesFontLeading and -setUsesFontLeading: (YES by
default) to the layout manager, and pass the string drawing option to
the layout manager string drawing uses.  With it the layout of every case
measured above matches macOS to the thousandth of a point, apart from
paragraph spacing, which this leaves as it was.

The test lays text out in the user font at two sizes, and in Arial,
Liberation Sans, Times New Roman and Liberation Serif where installed,
and checks each rule above against the font's own metrics.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
